      "info": {
        "contact": {
          "url": "https://html2pdf.fly.dev/"
        },
        "description": "This API allows users to convert any HTML source code into a downloadable PDF file. It is a free service that requires no authentication and can be easily integrated into applications using a simple HTTP POST request.",
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "Production server",
            "url": "https://html2pdf.fly.dev/api"
          }
        ],
        "title": "HTML to PDF Converter",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "tagline": "Turn raw HTML source into a downloadable PDF with a single HTTP POST, no signup required",
        "homepage": "https://html2pdf.fly.dev/",
        "docs_url": "https://html2pdf.fly.dev/",
        "license_short": "Free to use; no licence terms published on the service page.",
        "license_md": "- The hosted service is offered free of charge with no authentication or signup.\n- No formal terms of service, SLA, or licence text are published on the API page.\n- The underlying implementation is the open-source [puppeteer-html-to-pdf-converter](https://github.com/ideadapt/puppeteer-html-to-pdf-converter) project; check its repository for source-level licensing.\n- Treat the service as best-effort and confirm acceptable use before relying on it for production traffic.",
        "about_md": "[HTML to PDF Converter](https://html2pdf.fly.dev/) is a small hosted utility that turns a chunk of HTML source code into a PDF file. It runs on Fly.io and wraps the open-source [puppeteer-html-to-pdf-converter](https://github.com/ideadapt/puppeteer-html-to-pdf-converter) project, which uses headless Chromium under the hood.\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n\n- A single `POST /api/generate` endpoint that accepts a JSON body with an `html` field containing the markup to render.\n- A binary `application/pdf` response on success, or a JSON error payload on failure.\n- No API key, OAuth, or account — requests can be issued straight from a server-side script or job.\n\nOperationally the service is unauthenticated and has no documented rate limits, so it is best suited to low-volume conversions, prototypes, and internal tooling. CORS is disabled on the public endpoint, so calls should be made from a backend rather than directly from a browser.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "pdf_generation": "Render-on-demand PDFs produced from supplied HTML markup via `POST /api/generate`."
        },
